  abstract = {Generative researchers agree that an innate, domain-specific mental system constrains first language (L1) acquisition, but they disagree about whether it is involved in adult second language (L2) acquisition (e.g., Bley-Vroman, 1990; Schwartz \& Sprouse, 1996). This study investigates whether adult L1-Chinese speakers can become sensitive to a syntactic constraint in English that is not present in Chinese, not taught explicitly, and not learnable from the input alone using domain-general operations. If they can overcome this poverty-of-the-stimulus (POS) problem, it would suggest that the domain-specific system does guide adult L2 acquisition. The phenomenon at issue is the Adjunct Island Constraint, which prohibits extraction from an adjunct clause (Ross, 1967). While English sentences with topicalization exhibit clear adjunct island effects, the status of island constraints in Chinese, a topic-prominent language (Li \& Thompson, 1981), is a matter of ongoing debate (e.g., Huang, 1984; Xu, 1986). To test for sensitivity to adjunct island effects in English in a heretofore unexplored construction―topicalization―in this brand of L2 research, a contextualized acceptability judgment task was administered to adult L1-Chinese L2 learners (L2ers) of English and native speakers of English; the L2ers also completed a closely-translated Chinese version of the task so that comparisons between their L1 and L2 performance could be made. While both groups of participants showed clear signs of adjunct island effects on the English task, the L1-Chinese speakers did not exhibit any such effects on the Chinese task. These results suggest that Chinese native speakers are able to overcome the POS problem that they face in developing a sensitivity to the Adjunct Island Constraint in English, in support of the hypothesis that a domain-specific mental system constrains adult L2 acquisition.},
